Good — below is a focused, practical checklist and a set of questions you can take to Amy Foster so you’re ready for the preliminary hearing and next steps. I’ve grouped items so you can prioritize what to ask now, what to ask about likely judge questions, what opposing counsel may ask, and how to prepare answers and documents.

High‑priority questions to ask your attorney now
- What is the exact deadline for my response (answer/motion) and can it be extended? What happens if we miss it?  
- What are the immediate next filings we should make (answer, motion to dismiss, motion for more definite statement, etc.)? Pros/cons of each?  
- What should I expect at the preliminary hearing (purpose, typical length, rulings the judge may make)?  
- Do we need to seek a continuance? If so, what’s the best basis? How do we request it?  
- What is our overall strategy (settle, defend to verdict, narrow issues, compel arbitration, other)? What are the likely outcomes?  
- What evidence/documents do you need from me immediately? Preferred format (scanned, originals, email threads)?  
- Who are likely witnesses (for us and for them) and when should we prepare/meet with them?  
- Are there immediate discovery steps we should start (preservation letters, subpoenas, interrogatories, document requests)?  
- What are the deadlines and steps specific to Maricopa County Civil Division we should track (case management, disclosures, settlement conferences)?  
- Cost and fee estimates for the next phases (discovery, hearing, trial). Are there cost-saving options (mediation/arbitration)?

Questions to ask about how the judge might question you
- What types of questions will the judge ask at the preliminary hearing? (e.g., readiness for trial, status of discovery, jurisdiction, service issues)  
- How should I respond if the judge asks whether I admit liability or ask for more time to answer?  
- If the judge asks if I’m represented, what’s the best short answer? If I speak, what tone/length is appropriate?  
- What to do if the judge asks me to summarize my defense in a few sentences? Can we prepare a short script?  
- How to request or respond to the court if I need a continuance, more discovery, or mediation—what language should I use?  
- What are common procedural pitfalls at Maricopa preliminary hearings (waiving defenses, missing deadlines, failing to preserve objections)?

Questions to ask about what opposing counsel might ask you (or try to get from the court)
- What specific factual admissions might they try to elicit (dates, communications, signature, knowledge of NDA terms)?  
- How will opposing counsel try to frame the breach (intentional breach, willful disclosure, damages)? What facts will they emphasize?  
- Could they seek injunctive relief or emergency relief? How do we respond if they ask the judge for that?  
- What documents will they present and how might they try to authenticate them (emails, metadata, signed agreements)?  
- What lines of questioning might be used to attack my credibility or motives (prior disputes, inconsistent statements, financial motive)?  
- Are there traps in agreeing to a “stipulation” or “admission” in court? When should I refuse to answer without counsel present?  
- How might they approach settlement talk in open court and how should I respond?

How to prepare answers and what to say (practice tips)
- Prepare a 2–3 sentence “case summary” you can say if asked: who you are, high-level position (e.g., “I dispute the creditor’s claim that I breached the NDA because …”), and a one-line status (e.g., “We intend to defend and are requesting time for discovery.”) Review this with Amy.  
- Practice short neutral responses: “I’m represented by Amy Foster of Caldwell Legal,” “I decline to comment without counsel,” or “I need my attorney to respond to that.” Ask Amy which to use and when.  
- Avoid volunteering extra details. If you do not know, say so (“I don’t recall; I will confirm with my counsel”).  
- Work with Amy in a short mock Q&A / role play on likely lines of questioning.

Concrete documents and materials to bring or assemble before the hearing
- The signed NDA (all versions) and any amendments. Highlight disputed clauses.  
- Chronological timeline of events: service dates, communications, alleged breach dates, specific disclosures. Keep it one page plus a detailed backup.  
- All communications relevant to the NDA: emails, texts, internal notes, draft agreements, any correspondence with the creditor. Include metadata if available.  
- Any payment records, invoices, receipts, or bank records tied to the dispute (if damages are contested).  
- Proof of service and any court filings you’ve already received/sent.  
- Witness list and short summaries of what each witness will say. Contact info if needed.  
- Any offers, settlement communications, or mediation proposals.  
- Notes of your recollection, including dates you reviewed documents and who else had access.

Evidence and discovery issues to ask your attorney about
- What evidence do we need to authenticate (signatures, email headers, file metadata)? How do we secure it?  
- Should we send a litigation hold / preservation notice? To whom and how soon?  
- Are there privileged communications we should assert? How to mark them and avoid waiver?  
- Potential for subpoenas (documents or testimony): who to subpoena and when?  
- Are there expert witnesses we may need (forensic examiner, damages expert)? When should we retain them?

Evidentiary and tactical objections to discuss
- How to object to hearsay, foundation, lack of authentication, or relevance in the hearing/trial.  
- Whether any documents are best introduced through witnesses versus stipulation.  
- How to respond if opposing counsel tries to get you to “admit” elements of the NDA or damages in open court.

Settlement and alternative dispute resolution (ADR)
- Is mediation or settlement likely appropriate before trial? When to propose it?  
- How to evaluate any settlement offers — what factors should we ask the judge to consider before recommending settlement?  
- Confidentiality concerns: if settlement talks happen, how will confidentiality be protected (court-ordered sealing, protective order)?

Practical courtroom and conduct questions
- What should I wear and bring to the courthouse? (IDs, parking, courtroom decorum)  
- When do I need to arrive? Where in Maricopa County Civil Division should I check in? (Ask Amy for specific courtroom/time information.)  
- What to do if opposing counsel speaks to me privately at the courthouse.

Closing checklist to bring to your meeting with Amy
- One-page timeline and one-page “factual summary” for Amy to keep handy.  
- Copies of NDA and key communications (emails/texts).  
- List of questions from this checklist prioritized by importance.  
- Contact info for witnesses and any relevant third parties.  
- Your availability for hearings, depositions, and meetings.

Optional: quick script to ask Amy at your meeting
- “Amy, what are the three most important things I should say or avoid saying at the preliminary hearing?”  
- “Which documents should I produce immediately, and which should we hold until after we consult you?”  
- “If the judge asks me to admit facts on the record, how should I respond?”  
- “What are our best short‑term procedural options and their deadlines?”

