Good — this is exactly the right time to get organized. Below I’ve put together (1) immediate steps you should take now, (2) a prioritized list of questions to ask Amy Foster, (3) likely questions a judge or opposing counsel will ask at a preliminary hearing or early stages, (4) suggested short answers / how to prepare, and (5) a document/witness checklist you can assemble and bring to your meeting. You can pick and choose which parts to ask Amy or rehearse with her.

1) Immediate steps (do these now)
- Preserve everything: don’t delete emails, texts, voicemails, drafts, notes, documents, invoices, or calendar entries related to the NDA or the creditor. Save originals or export copies (PDFs/screenshots) with dates.
- Stop discussing the case publicly or with third parties (including social media). Tell anyone who might be a witness to preserve their records.
- Gather the complaint and proof of service and note all deadlines (response date, hearing date).
- Provide Amy with everything (signed NDA, related contracts, communications, payment records, work product, notes, witnesses’ names and contact info).
- Ask Amy about filing a responsive pleading or motion and confirm who will file it and when so you don’t miss the deadline.

2) Questions to ask Amy Foster (organized by topic)
Procedural / immediate
- What are the exact deadlines (response to complaint, pretrial deadlines, discovery deadlines, preliminary hearing date) and what should I expect next?
- What happens at the preliminary hearing in Maricopa County Civil Division? What will the judge typically ask us to do?
- Do we need to move to dismiss, answer the complaint, or ask for more time? What are the pros/cons of each option?
- Is there a risk of default judgment if we miss a deadline? How do we avoid that?

Case strategy & likely outcomes
- What is our overall defense strategy to the creditor’s claims (e.g., no breach, NDA invalid/unenforceable, lack of damages, consent, waiver)?
- What do you see as our strongest and weakest points?
- What are realistic outcomes: dismissal, settlement, damages, trial? Likelihood estimate and timeline?
- Should we consider settlement/mediation now or wait until after discovery?

Evidence & discovery
- What documents and communications will be most important for discovery and for trial?
- Should we seek a protective order to limit what’s disclosed about confidential info?
- What documents should I proactively produce? What should we withhold as privileged?
- Are there any preservation steps or spoliation issues I should be aware of?

Witnesses & testimony
- Who should we plan to call as witnesses? Do we need expert testimony?
- Will I need to be deposed? How should I prepare for a deposition or cross-examination?
- What questions are likely at deposition or in court; what answers do you want me to give/avoid?

Costs, fees, and risks
- What are estimated costs to litigate through discovery and trial versus settling?
- Are there fee-shifting risks (e.g., attorney fees if they prevail on the NDA)?
- Do we have exposure to punitive damages or other non-contractual remedies?

Tactical / motions
- Should we file any early motions (motion to dismiss, motion to strike, motion for more definite statement, change of venue)?
- Should we assert counterclaims or affirmative defenses (e.g., unconscionability, ambiguity, fraud, lack of consideration, waiver, mitigation)?
- When and how should we seek injunctive relief (if needed) or oppose their injunctive requests?

Communications & conduct
- How should I communicate with the opposing party or their counsel (email only through you? no direct contact?)?
- What should I say if opposing counsel calls or emails me directly?

Trial prep & logistics
- What’s the expected trial schedule and discovery timeline in Maricopa County civil cases?
- How long will trial likely take and what resources do we need (exhibits, demonstratives, witnesses)?
- How do we handle confidential exhibits in court — sealing, redaction, or in camera submissions?

3) Likely questions a judge will ask at a preliminary hearing
(These are general and common in initial scheduling/preliminary hearings)
- Are the parties represented? (Appearance of counsel)
- Has the defendant been properly served?
- Does the defendant intend to file an answer or a dispositive motion?
- Are there jurisdiction or venue issues?
- Are the parties ready to set a discovery schedule and a trial date? (or request mediation)
- Is there a request for an extension of time to respond?
- Any pending emergency issues (injunctions, preservation orders)?
- Estimated length of trial and whether trial by jury is demanded.

4) Likely questions from opposing counsel (and how to prepare)
They will try to pin down the facts that support their claim and your weaknesses. Expect questions about:
- The NDA itself — when was it signed, who signed it, was it executed properly, what are its exact terms and scope (time, geography, types of information)?
- The alleged breach — what specific act or disclosure are they claiming, when did it happen, and how did you allegedly disclose confidential info?
- Your intent — did you knowingly disclose or misuse confidential information?
- Damages — what harm or loss resulted from the alleged breach; can they quantify damages?
- Mitigation — did you take steps to mitigate damages or return/confine the info?
- Authorization — did anyone else authorize disclosure or waive the NDA?
- Prior communications — any inconsistent statements, admissions, or prior emails that contradict your current position.

How to prepare answers (short guidance)
- Stick to facts and dates. Avoid speculation or giving unnecessary detail.
- If you don’t recall, say so and offer to check records. Don’t guess.
- Don’t volunteer extra information that isn’t asked for.
- Coordinate with Amy on a short factual timeline you can rely on.
- Rehearse brief truthful answers to common questions (who, what, when, where, why).

5) Document and evidence checklist to assemble
Essential documents
- The NDA (signed copy) and any related agreements or amendments.
- The complaint, summons, and proof of service you were served with.
- Any written communications about the NDA (emails, texts, letters) between you and the creditor or third parties.
- Payment records, invoices, or proof of performance related to the underlying relationship.
- Notes, calendars, meeting minutes, and drafts relevant to the dispute.
- Personnel or third-party agreements that reference the NDA or confidentiality obligations.
- Any documents the creditor relied on (if you have copies) or that they claim were disclosed.
- Screenshots, saved web pages, files, or metadata showing transmissions or access.
- Backup files, cloud logs, or device logs (preserve — do not overwrite).

Witnesses
- Names and contact info for anyone who can testify about facts: who signed the NDA, who saw/received a communication, who can corroborate your account.
- Expert names (if applicable) or whether an expert will be needed for damages or technical matters.

Privilege & sensitive items
- Identify communications with your attorney (do not produce without checking with Amy) — mark as privileged.
- Flag any highly confidential trade secrets; discuss protective order options.

6) Prep for deposition/court answers — suggested short scripts
- “I don’t recall the exact date; I will check my records and provide that.” (better than guessing)
- “I don’t want to speculate. My counsel will verify that.” (when unsure of legal significance)
- “I’ve preserved all communications and documents related to this matter and will provide them to my attorney.” (shows cooperation)

7) Next actions I recommend you take and ask Amy right away
- Confirm response filing strategy (answer vs motion) and who will file.
- Deliver all documents and witness names to Amy now; create a simple timeline (dates, events, documents).
- Ask Amy to outline the discovery plan and immediate protective measures (e.g., preservation letters, protective order).
- Schedule a prep meeting before any deposition or hearing to practice answers.

If you want, I can:
- Help you draft a clear chronological timeline of events to give to Amy (list dates + one-sentence descriptions).
- Create a tailored checklist/email you can send Amy listing documents you’ve gathered and any gaps.
- Draft short practice Q&A scripts for common deposition or courtroom questions.

Do you want me to help you build the timeline or draft that checklist/email to send to Amy Foster? If so, tell me the key dates/events and what documents you already have.

