Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Amy Foster. Below I’ve put together (A) immediate priorities, (B) a focused list of questions to ask Amy (grouped by topic), (C) likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask so you can prepare answers, (D) documents and materials to bring to meetings/hearings, and (E) practical courtroom / testimony tips. I’m not a lawyer — this is preparation help only. Run everything with Amy before acting.

A. Immediate priorities
- Confirm with Amy the deadline to respond to the complaint and whether you need to file anything now (answer, motion, appearance). Missing a deadline can have serious consequences.
- Ask whether the upcoming “preliminary hearing” is a scheduling/initial status hearing, a hearing on temporary relief (e.g., injunction or attachment), or something else.
- Identify whether discovery is open, and what immediate preservation steps you must take (documents, devices, communications).

B. Questions to ask Amy Foster (grouped)

Case status & deadlines
- What exactly happens at the upcoming preliminary hearing? (purpose, likely length, witnesses, relief sought)
- What is the deadline for my formal response (answer/motion)? If needed, can we get an extension and how?
- What court rules/timetable will govern discovery, motions, and trial in Maricopa County civil division for this case?

Procedural risk & strategy
- What defenses do you recommend asserting (e.g., no breach, agreement unenforceable, lack of consideration, scope ambiguity, statute of limitations, waiver, estoppel)?
- Should we consider any counterclaims or affirmative defenses?
- Is there risk of a default judgment if I miss a deadline or fail to appear? How can we avoid that?

Evidence & discovery
- What documents and communications are most important to preserve and produce? (emails, texts, drafts of the NDA, payment records, work product)
- What are likely discovery requests (interrogatories, requests for production, depositions)? How should I prepare?
- Do we need to subpoena third-party records (bank records, cloud provider, phone carrier)? When?

Witnesses & testimony
- Will I be expected to testify at hearings or trial? If so, should I prepare a declaration or affidavit?
- Do we plan to call witnesses? Who should we consider (co-workers, supervisors, recipients of the allegedly confidential information)?
- Should we prepare experts (e.g., damages, industry practice, forensic analysis)?

Specific NDA issues to cover
- How does the NDA define “confidential information”? Is it clear or overbroad?
- Was the NDA validly formed (signature, consideration, scope, duration)?
- Are there carve-outs (public info, independently developed, permitted disclosures)?
- How would the plaintiff prove damages and causation for the alleged breach?

Tactical / urgent motions
- Should we move to dismiss or file a special motion to strike (e.g., anti-SLAPP if applicable)?
- Any reason to seek a protective order or motion in limine now?
- Should we seek to bifurcate issues (liability vs damages) or seek a stay?

Settlement & costs
- What’s your assessment of settlement prospects and reasonable settlement range?
- What are estimated litigation costs and timeline to trial?
- If settlement talks begin, who will handle negotiations and confidentiality of offers?

Communications & confidentiality
- What should I say (and not say) to opposing counsel, third parties, and on social media?
- Are there any confidentiality or non-disclosure obligations that limit what I can share with friends/family? Should we request a protective order?

Privilege & testimony control
- When can I assert privilege? Which communications are protected (attorney-client, work product)?
- If asked questions in deposition or hearing that may incriminate me, what are our options? (Discuss Fifth Amendment implications with Amy — in civil cases invoking the Fifth can have consequences.)

Practical preparation
- Can you review key documents before the hearing? How much time do you need?
- Would you like me to prepare a written statement, timeline, or witness list?
- Do you recommend a mock prep session or role play before the hearing?

C. Likely questions you should prepare to answer
(Practice short, factual answers. Don’t volunteer extra info; check with Amy before making legal statements.)

From opposing counsel (or their witness):
- Did you sign the NDA? When and where?
- What did you understand the NDA to cover?
- What was the nature of your relationship with the plaintiff/creditor at the time?
- What specific information did you (allegedly) disclose? To whom and when?
- Did you share any documents, emails, or messages? Provide details.
- Did you receive any compensation or benefit tied to the NDA? (Consideration)
- Were any versions or drafts of the NDA exchanged? Did you negotiate terms?
- Did you attempt to remedy or mitigate any alleged disclosure? (e.g., recall request)
- Have you discussed this case with third parties or on social media?
- Can you identify witnesses or documents that support your account?

From the judge at a preliminary/scheduling hearing:
- Are you represented? (state yes and confirm Amy Foster is counsel)
- What issues do you anticipate needing time for (discovery, motions, experts)?
- Do you object to any pending motions or scheduling proposals?
- Do you require emergency relief or outstanding discovery?
- Are you ready to discuss settlement or ADR?

Likely factual points to be probed:
- Timeline: when NDA executed, when alleged breach occurred, when plaintiff discovered it, when they sued.
- Scope: what information is claimed confidential and why it is confidential.
- Damages: what loss or harm plaintiff alleges and what proof they have.

D. Documents and materials to bring / preserve
Bring originals and organized copies (and provide digital copies to your lawyer).
- Complaint, summons, any court filings, and proof of service
- The NDA — all versions, drafts, emails about negotiations
- All communications with the plaintiff/creditor (emails, texts, messages, Teams/Slack)
- Documents allegedly disclosed (if any), and context showing whether they were public or widely shared
- Work files, deliverables, invoices, timesheets, project notes
- Financial records showing any alleged damages or payments
- Dates/timeline: a concise chronological timeline you’ve prepared
- Witness list and contact information
- Phone and device logs (if relevant), metadata, backup files
- Any prior settlement offers, releases, or related agreements
- Correspondence with counsel (yours or theirs) and privileged notes (review with Amy before sharing)

E. Courtroom and testimony tips
- Address the judge as “Your Honor.” Speak clearly and briefly.
- Answer only the question asked. Keep answers short and factual. Don’t guess.
- If you don’t remember, say you don’t remember rather than guessing.
- If you’re unsure whether to answer (privilege, Fifth, legal strategy), pause and ask to consult with Amy before answering.
- Correct mistakes immediately if you realize you misspoke; say “I misstated — the correct date is…”
- Dress conservatively and arrive early. Bring all documents and a notebook.
- Do not post about the case on social media; avoid discussing the case with non-essential third parties.

F. Practice and next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email or checklist to send to Amy listing your questions/concerns.
- Create a one-page timeline and bullet list of facts you can review with Amy.
- Role-play likely deposition/hearing questions so you can practice short answers.
- Draft suggested language for responses to common questions (non-binding templates you’ll review with Amy).

If you want, I can:
- Draft an email to Amy with the prioritized list of questions above.
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in.
- Role-play a mock Q&A for the preliminary hearing (I’ll play opposing counsel/judge; you practice answers).

Which of those would you like me to prepare next?

