Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Amy. Below I’ve put together: (A) focused questions you should ask your attorney now, (B) the kinds of questions the judge is likely to ask at a preliminary hearing or status conference, (C) the kinds of questions opposing counsel is likely to ask early on, (D) a document/evidence checklist to bring, and (E) practical preparation and courtroom/testimony tips. Use these with Amy Foster (Caldwell Legal) and ask her to role‑play possible lines of questioning with you.

A. Questions to ask your attorney (prioritize these in your meeting)
- Procedural / deadlines
  - What exact deadlines apply to my case (deadline to respond to the complaint, discovery cutoffs, hearing dates, motion deadlines) in Maricopa County? Can any deadlines be extended?
  - What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing (status conference, scheduling, temporary relief, other)?
  - What filings should we prepare before the hearing?
- Case posture & strategy
  - What is our immediate strategy (dismissal, defense, counterclaim, settlement)? What are the pros and cons of each?
  - What defenses do you think are strongest and why? What risks do we face?
  - What outcomes are realistic at this stage (dismissal, summary judgment, settlement range, trial)?
- Evidence & witnesses
  - What documents or communications will be most important to support our position about the NDA?
  - Who should be witnesses for us (people with direct knowledge, custodians of records, experts)? When and how will they be prepared or deposed?
  - Do we need expert witnesses (e.g., damages or industry practice)? If so, when must they be retained and disclosed?
- Discovery & motions
  - What discovery should we seek right away (requests for production, interrogatories, admissions, depositions)?
  - Should we file any immediate motions (motion to dismiss, motion to strike, motion for protective order, motion for extension)?
- Settlement & ADR
  - Is mediation or another ADR option appropriate now? When would we consider it?
  - What settlement authority will you need from me (minimum acceptable terms)?
- Practical & administrative
  - What should I say or not say to opposing counsel, to third parties, or on social media?
  - How should I preserve evidence now (emails, phone logs, devices, backups)?
  - Are there privilege or confidentiality issues I need to be careful about?
- Costs & logistics
  - What are estimated legal fees and costs through the next stage (preliminary hearing, discovery, trial)?
  - How often will we communicate and what do you need from me before next meeting?
- Courtroom preparation
  - Can we do a mock Q&A/cross-examination before the hearing? Can you outline the short facts I should be ready to state on the record?
  - How do we handle any questions from the judge that require legal argument vs. factual answers?

B. Questions a judge is likely to ask at a preliminary hearing / status conference
(These are general — judges often focus on case management and the narrow issues for that hearing.)
- Have you been properly served? Has an answer been filed?
- Are there any pending motions or emergency requests?
- What is the current schedule you propose for discovery, motions, and trial?
- Will the parties agree to a case management plan, or is a scheduling order needed?
- Are there disputes about service, jurisdiction, venue, or pleadings that need resolution now?
- Are the parties willing to try ADR/mediation? If so, when?
- Are there requests for a protective order or confidentiality concerns (particularly in NDA cases)?
- Is any party seeking temporary relief or injunctions?
- Are there issues affecting trial readiness (witness availability, experts, voluminous documents)?
- Do the parties anticipate any evidentiary or privilege disputes?
Ask Amy how she would answer each of the above and what you should say on the record.

C. Questions opposing counsel is likely to ask (early discovery, depositions, or at hearing)
Opposing counsel will try to develop facts that support the creditor’s claim and undermine defenses:
- Did you sign the NDA? When and in what circumstances?
- What was the purpose of the NDA and what information was covered?
- Did you disclose the confidential information—when, to whom, and how?
- What communications occurred between you and the opposing party about the NDA?
- Were there any agreements, amendments, or prior drafts of the NDA?
- Did you receive consideration for the NDA? Were you coerced or pressured into signing?
- What damages has the opposing party suffered as a result of the alleged breach?
- What steps did you take to mitigate any alleged damage?
- Who else had access to the information and why?
- Do you contest the authenticity or completeness of any documents they produced?
- Do you have any counterclaims or setoffs (e.g., payments, breaches by the other party)?
Tell Amy which of these you think could hurt you so she can prepare you for how to respond.

D. Document & evidence checklist to bring to your meeting
Bring originals or copies (organized chronologically if possible):
- The written NDA(s), all pages, and any versions/drafts
- Signature pages, dates, and identities of signatories
- All communications (emails, texts, instant messages, letters) related to the NDA and the dispute
- Correspondence with the creditor and any collection letters or demands
- The complaint and proof of service, summons, and any court notices
- Any responses you already filed or drafts of a responsive pleading
- Payment records, invoices, business records related to the subject matter of the NDA
- Records showing who had access to the allegedly confidential information
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, or minutes supporting your timeline
- Witness names, contact info, and short summaries of what they saw or know
- Any prior agreements, employment contracts, consulting agreements, or related NDAs
- Screenshots, backups, or metadata if electronic files are relevant
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about settlement
- Insurance policies that might cover defense/costs
Bring an annotated timeline (one page) summarizing key dates and events — that’s extremely helpful for your attorney.

E. Practical preparation and testimony tips (what to do / not do)
- Do: Be concise, truthful, and stick to facts you know. Practice short, clear answers.
- Don’t: Volunteer extra information; do not guess — if you don’t know, say you don’t know.
- Practice: Ask Amy to do a mock direct and cross-examination so you’re comfortable answering under pressure.
- Evidence: Bring organized copies of exhibits (and labels) you may want to admit; ask Amy to prepare an exhibit list.
- Privilege: Ask Amy what you can discuss with third parties and what remains privileged.
- Preservation: Immediately preserve all potentially relevant evidence; don’t delete or alter files.
- Communications: Avoid contacting opposing counsel or the opposing party directly without Amy’s approval.
- On the record: If a question requires legal analysis, you can (and should) ask to confer with your attorney before answering substantive legal questions.
- Social media: Don’t post about the case or related events on social media.

F. Practical next steps for your meeting with Amy
- Send Amy the checklist items and the one‑page timeline before the meeting.
- Ask for a prioritized list of what you must do before the hearing (documents to bring, motions to file).
- Schedule a mock Q&A and plan for how you’ll present your core facts in 1–2 minutes.
- Confirm deadlines and whether Amy will file an appearance and answer if not already done.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a one‑page timeline template you can fill in with dates and summaries.
- Draft a short list of suggested answers (non‑legal, factual phrasing) to common questions you expect to face so you can rehearse with Amy.
- Help you prepare a meeting agenda to send to Amy so your time is most productive.

