Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Amy. Below I’ll give (A) a practical list of focused questions to ask your attorney now, (B) likely questions a judge or opposing counsel might ask you at the preliminary hearing or later, (C) a short prep checklist (documents, witnesses, timeline) to bring to your lawyer and court, and (D) brief practice tips for answering questions and for the hearing. I’m not your lawyer, so treat this as preparation and discussion points to take to Amy Foster.

A — Questions to ask your attorney (bring these up at your next meeting)
- Deadlines & procedure
  - Exactly how long do I have to file a response/answer in Maricopa County for this case? What is the deadline and how must it be filed and served?
  - What happens if we miss that deadline? Are extensions available and how do we request one?
  - What will happen at the preliminary hearing? What rulings or actions can the judge take there?

- Case theory & defenses
  - What is our legal theory/defense to the creditor’s claims (contract invalidity, no breach, scope of NDA, payment/consideration, etc.)?
  - What affirmative defenses should I raise (statute of limitations, improper service, lack of standing, waiver, estoppel, ambiguity, illegality, etc.)?
  - Are there any weaknesses in our position we should address proactively?

- Evidence & witnesses
  - What documents and evidence are essential for our defense? How should they be organized for Amy and for court?
  - Which witnesses should we plan to call, and what will they need to testify about?
  - Any forensic or expert evidence needed (for example, email metadata, IT logs, accountant)?

- Discovery & motions
  - What is the discovery schedule likely to be? What initial discovery requests should we prepare or expect?
  - Should we consider an early motion (motion to dismiss, to strike, for more definite statement, to compel arbitration, etc.)?
  - Are there strategic benefits to moving to dismiss vs. answering?

- Settlement & risk assessment
  - What settlement options exist? What would be an acceptable settlement range (financial and nonfinancial)?
  - What are likely court costs and attorney fees if this goes to trial? What are the realistic best/worst case outcomes?

- Hearing logistics & representation
  - Will you represent me at the preliminary hearing and at trial if necessary? Who will attend with you?
  - What should I say (or avoid saying) at the hearing? Will I need to testify at this stage?
  - How do we handle any communication from opposing counsel (letters, calls, settlement offers)?

- Administrative and billing
  - What are your fees for the preliminary hearing and for trial preparation? Any likely additional costs (experts, depositions)?
  - What do you need from me right now (documents, timeline, contacts)?

B — Questions you should expect from the judge or opposing counsel
(Practice short, factual answers. Don’t guess — say “I don’t recall” or “I need to confer with my attorney” if unsure.)

- Identity & status
  - Can you state your name for the record?
  - Are you represented by counsel?

- Procedural
  - Have you been served with the complaint? When were you served?
  - Have you filed an answer or any response? If not, why or when will you?

- Factual – NDA & dispute specifics
  - Did you sign the NDA? On what date?
  - Who signed the NDA on behalf of the other party?
  - What were the confidentiality obligations under the NDA (what was protected)?
  - What exactly did you allegedly disclose or fail to do?
  - Where/when did the alleged disclosure or breach occur (emails, phone, meetings)?
  - Did you receive any written warnings or notices about the alleged breach?

- Contract formation & performance
  - Did you receive any consideration for the NDA? (How/when were you paid or compensated?)
  - Were any modifications, amendments, or terminations made to the NDA in writing? Were any oral agreements made?
  - Did the other party perform their obligations? Were there breaches by them?

- Damages & mitigation
  - What harm has the creditor alleged? Do you have evidence (financial records, invoices) to dispute or quantify damages?
  - Did you take any steps to mitigate damages after the alleged breach?

- Communications & witnesses
  - Have you communicated with the creditor about this dispute? Do you have emails/texts?
  - Who else was present during the events in question? Can they testify?

- Intent & knowledge
  - Did you knowingly or intentionally disclose confidential information?
  - Did you believe the information was confidential or already public?

- Settlement & willingness to resolve
  - Are you willing to participate in settlement discussions or mediation?

C — Document & evidence checklist to bring to Amy and to the hearing
Bring organized, chronologically labeled copies (paper and electronic) and a one-page timeline summary.

Essential documents:
- The signed NDA (original or copy), including all pages and amendments
- The creditor’s complaint and any attachments
- Proof of service papers
- Any written communications between you and the creditor relating to the NDA or dispute (emails, texts, letters)
- Any communications with third parties that are relevant
- Payment records, invoices, receipts, or evidence of consideration
- Notes or contemporaneous logs of meetings or phone calls (dates, participants, content)
- Documents that show whether the information was public or previously disclosed
- Any offers to settle, demand letters, or responses
- Identification and contact info for witnesses
- Expert reports, accounting records, or metadata if available (email headers, timestamps, logs)

Create a concise timeline (one page) listing dates and what happened — this helps Amy and the judge understand the sequence quickly.

D — Quick tips for answering questions and courtroom behavior
- Be concise and truthful. Answer only what’s asked; don’t volunteer extra detail.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so. “I don’t recall” is better than inaccurate guessing.
- Avoid arguing with opposing counsel or the judge. Let your attorney object or intercede.
- Speak clearly, audibly, and address the judge as “Your Honor” when required.
- Don’t sign anything or agree to anything outside of counsel without consulting Amy.
- If approached by opposing counsel for an unsupervised conversation, decline and say you’ll speak through your attorney.
- Practice short, direct answers to the likely factual questions above with Amy (role-play).

Optional next steps I can help with right now
- Draft a one-page timeline from the facts you give me.
- Create a printable list of the key questions for Amy.
- Role-play likely questioning (I can act as opposing counsel/judge and ask you practice questions).

Would you like me to (1) draft the one-page timeline checklist for you to give Amy, or (2) create a printable question list to bring to the hearing, or (3) role-play a short mock questioning session? If yes, give me key dates and a short sequence of events (when NDA was signed, when you were served, main disputed events).

