   - Opposing counsel/judge: “What damages does the plaintiff claim/what damages do you dispute?” — Know plaintiff’s claimed damages and our position; bring evidence undermining damages (timelines, valuation, contemporaneous notes).
   - Preparation: Collect financial records, communications about value or usage of alleged information.

4. Questions about intent and knowledge
   - Opposing counsel: “Did you intentionally disclose the information?” — Be honest. If disclosure was inadvertent or minimal, prepare to explain context succinctly.
   - Preparation: Prepare a short timeline showing intent and contacts to show lack of willful misconduct if applicable.

5. Credibility and inconsistent statements
   - Opposing counsel will try to show contradictions: “Earlier you said X; now you say Y.” — Review all prior statements, emails, texts, and deposition (if any) with Amy to ensure consistency.
   - Preparation: Make a timeline of events and review it with Amy to avoid contradictions.

6. Discovery and production questions
   - Opposing counsel/judge: “Have you preserved relevant evidence?” — You should be able to say yes; tell Amy about any deletion/cleanup that happened — full disclosure to counsel is critical.
   - Preparation: Do not delete documents or messages. Provide a list of devices/accounts with potentially relevant data.

C. How to behave if you testify (practical tips)
- Keep answers short and factual — don’t volunteer extra information.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so (“I don’t recall”) rather than guessing.
- Avoid arguing with the opposing counsel or judge. Be calm and measured.
- Review potential cross-examination lines with Amy; do mock questioning if possible.
- Don’t change your story from earlier statements; if prior statements differ, tell Amy and prepare an explanation.

D. Documents and items to bring to your meeting and hearings
- Complaint, summons, all court filings, and any correspondence from the creditor or court.
- The NDA itself and any versions or related agreements.
- All communications (emails, text messages, messages, notes, meeting minutes) involving the NDA or alleged disclosure.
