Good—I can help you get organized and ready. Below I’ve put together (A) the key questions to ask your attorney now, (B) the likely questions the judge or opposing counsel may ask at the preliminary hearing or in early proceedings, with short suggested responses you can practice, (C) a documents/evidence checklist to gather, and (D) practical tips for the hearing and next steps. If you want, I can also draft a short email to Amy Foster listing these questions.

A. Priority questions to ask your attorney (Amy Foster at Caldwell Legal)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the exact deadline to respond to the complaint (date and how it’s computed from the service date)?
  - Is there any immediate deadline or emergency relief I should be asking for (e.g., temporary restraining order, TRO)?
  - What are the next scheduled court dates (preliminary hearing, case management conference, deadlines for discovery)?

- Strategy & legal posture
  - What is our recommended immediate response (answer, motion to dismiss, motion to strike, request for more time)?
  - What are the strongest defenses to enforcement of this NDA (e.g., lack of consideration, overbreadth, ambiguous terms, public policy, prior release)?
  - Are there counterclaims or affirmative defenses we should assert now?

- Evidence & witness planning
  - What evidence will the court expect to see to defend against the creditor’s claims (contracts, communications, proof of non-disclosure or authorization, proof of returned/destroyed materials)?
  - Which witnesses are essential and when should we get witness statements or depositions scheduled?

- Discovery & preservation
  - What exactly must I preserve (phones, laptops, cloud accounts, documents)? Do we need a formal preservation letter to opposing counsel to avoid spoliation?
  - Should I stop communicating with certain people or companies? What communications are privileged?

- Risk & remedies
  - What damages or remedies is the creditor seeking (monetary damages, injunctive relief, attorneys’ fees)?
  - What is my exposure and best-case/worst-case outcomes?
  - What are settlement options and the likely cost-benefit of settlement vs. litigating?

- Court procedure & local rules
  - Are there Maricopa County or Arizona-specific local rules I must follow (filings, formatting, service rules)?
  - What should I expect at the preliminary hearing procedurally? Will the judge ask factual questions, set scheduling, or rule on motions?

- Practical logistics
  - Do you want me to prepare a written timeline/declaration? If so, what should it include and by when?
  - Who else on your team will handle discovery and court filings? How will we coordinate communications?
  - Should I retain any experts now (e.g., forensic, damages)?

B. Questions you should expect from the judge or opposing counsel — and short, safe practice responses
(Note: these are for preparation; always defer to your attorney for legal answers.)

Likely questions and short practice replies:
- “Did you sign the NDA?” — “Yes/No. (If yes) I signed on [date]; I can provide a copy.” — (If unsure) “I’ll need to confirm the exact date and terms; I have a copy I can provide.”
- “Do you admit you disclosed the information described in the complaint?” — “I don’t admit to that. I’ll let my attorney address the legal details, and I can provide factual information.”
- “What information did you disclose and to whom?” — Brief factual statement only; e.g., “I shared [document/email/evidence] with [person/company] on/around [date].”
- “Were you aware of the confidentiality obligations?” — “I understood there was a confidentiality requirement; I’ll explain the details with my attorney.”
- “Why did you disclose (if you did)?” — Stick to facts: “I believed the disclosure was permitted under [reason — e.g., prior authorization, public information, compelled by law]; I will provide documentation.”
- “Have you destroyed or altered any documents or devices?” — “No. I have preserved the relevant documents and devices and can provide them.”
- “Do you agree to a stay/extension/settlement?” — Only agree after discussing with your attorney; say: “I’d like to consult my attorney before answering.”
- From the judge: “Are the parties ready to proceed?” or “How much time do you need?” — “I would like to consult with my attorney on that scheduling matter.”

General tips on answering:
- Be truthful, concise, and avoid volunteering extra information.
- If you don’t know or don’t remember, say so: “I don’t recall” or “I’ll need to check the records.”
- If unsure how to answer legally, defer: “I’d like to consult my attorney before answering.”
- Avoid speculation (“I think,” “maybe”) on important facts.

C. Documents and evidence checklist to gather immediately
- The signed NDA (all versions) and any amendments, side letters, or related agreements.
- The complaint and all papers you were served with (summons, exhibits).
- All communications referencing the NDA or the alleged confidential information: emails, texts, DMs, letters, meeting notes.
- A dated timeline of events: when you signed the NDA, when alleged disclosures occurred, who was involved, and all dates.
- Copies of what was allegedly disclosed (if you have them), and evidence showing the information was public or independently known.
- Proof of consideration (payments, agreements) — anything showing the NDA was validly formed.
- Documents showing authorization or consent for disclosure if any (e.g., written permissions).
- Device/IT evidence: files, metadata, backups showing access/transfer timestamps.
- Financial records or invoices if the creditor claims damages tied to loss of revenue.
- Any prior releases, waivers, or conflicting agreements.
- Witness contact info and short written statements (if possible).
- Preservation copies: make forensic copies or at least exports of emails and chat logs; note where items are stored.

D. Practical hearing and preparation tips
- Confirm the purpose of the preliminary hearing (status conference vs. evidentiary hearing). Ask Amy to explain so you know whether the judge will take testimony or just set schedule.
- Meet with Amy beforehand to review your answers to likely questions; a 30–60 minute prep call is standard.
- Dress professionally, arrive early, bring hard copies of key documents, and bring a notepad to write directions.
- During the hearing, wait for your attorney to object or interpose; speak only when asked and after consulting if needed.
- Preserve everything: do not delete emails or files; document any steps you take to secure data.
- Consider whether to obtain a protective order if discovery will involve sensitive business information.
